1	# Syncbox
2	
3	Self-hosted file storage with sync: an HTTP server plus a CLI client.
4	The contract lives in [SYNCBOX-SPEC.md](SYNCBOX-SPEC.md) and
5	[syncbox-openapi.yaml](syncbox-openapi.yaml).
6	
7	Implementation: JavaScript on Node.js 20 with no npm dependencies. Everything
8	runs in Docker (`node:20-alpine`) through docker compose, so the host
9	only needs Docker with the compose plugin.
10	
11	## Running
12	
13	```sh
14	./run-server --data-dir ./data --port 8080   # foreground; Ctrl+C / SIGTERM stops it
15	curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/healthz           # {"status":"ok"}
16	curl -X PUT --data-binary @file.txt http://127.0.0.1:8080/blobs/docs/file.txt  # 201 {"key","sha256","size"}
17	curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/blobs/docs/file.txt                                # 200 + bytes, 404 if missing
18	curl -X DELETE http://127.0.0.1:8080/blobs/docs/file.txt                      # 204 no body, 404 if missing
19	curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/blobs             # 200 [{"key","size","sha256","modified_at"}], sorted by key; [] if empty
20	./run-client push ./mydir --server http://127.0.0.1:8080  # upload new and changed files
21	./run-client pull ./mydir --server http://127.0.0.1:8080  # download new and changed blobs
22	./run-tests                                  # full test suite, non-zero exit on failure
23	```
24	
25	`run-server` also reads `SYNCBOX_DATA_DIR` / `SYNCBOX_PORT`; flags take
26	precedence. The data directory is created if missing and bind-mounted into
27	the container; files are written with the invoking user's UID/GID.
28	
29	## Client
30	
31	`run-client <push|pull|status|sync> <dir> --server <url>` runs the `syncbox`
32	CLI (`bin/syncbox`) in a container. `--server` can also come from
33	`SYNCBOX_SERVER`; the flag takes precedence. `<dir>` must exist, except for
34	`pull`, which creates it. It is bind-mounted into the container, read-only
35	except for `pull` (compose services `client` and `client-writable`), and the
36	container runs as the invoking user, so files it writes belong to them. The container
37	shares the host's network, so `--server` means the same as on the host
38	(`http://127.0.0.1:<port>` reaches a server started by `run-server`).
39	
40	`push` walks `<dir>` recursively and keys each regular file by its relative
41	POSIX path (`docs/readme.txt`). It fetches `GET /blobs` and `PUT`s every file
42	whose SHA-256 is missing from the server or differs from the server's. Files
43	identical to the server's copy are not sent, and nothing is deleted on either
44	side. Symbolic links, special files and names that are not valid UTF-8 are
45	skipped with a note on stderr. stdout gets one `uploaded <key>` line per upload
46	and a summary.
47	
48	`pull` is the reverse: it fetches `GET /blobs` and `GET`s every blob that is
49	missing from `<dir>` or whose SHA-256 differs from the local file's, writing
50	it to the path named by its key and creating subdirectories as needed. Local
51	changes to such a file are overwritten. Files identical to the server's copy
52	are not fetched, and nothing is deleted on either side. Each download goes to
53	a temporary `.syncbox-*.part` file next to its target, is checked against the
54	SHA-256 from the listing, and is then renamed into place (keeping the
55	permissions of the file it replaces), so an interrupted pull leaves no
56	truncated files; on SIGINT/SIGTERM the temporary file is removed and the
57	exit code is `130`/`143`. Nothing is written outside `<dir>`: keys that are
58	not plain relative paths, and paths leading through or onto a symbolic link,
59	are skipped with a note on stderr. A directory where a file should go (or a
60	file where a directory should go) is a failure. stdout gets one
61	`downloaded <key>` line per download and a summary.
62	
63	Exit codes: `0` success, `1` failure (message on stderr; push and pull stop
64	at the first failure), `2` invalid arguments. `status` and `sync` are
65	accepted but not implemented yet: they exit `1` with a message saying so.
66	
67	`docker compose run` does not pass signals on to the container. So
68	`run-client` gives the client a TTY when it runs in a terminal, which lets
69	Ctrl+C reach the client itself. Without a terminal, the streams stay separate,
70	and a client whose wrapper is killed runs to completion.
71	
72	## Keys
73	
74	A key is a relative POSIX path, percent-decoded from the URL (`%2F` counts as
75	`/`). `PUT`, `GET` and `DELETE` answer `400` for a key that is empty, absolute,
76	contains an empty, `.` or `..` segment, a NUL, invalid UTF-8 or a lone
77	surrogate, or a segment over 255 bytes. Independently of that, the store
78	resolves every key to a path on disk and refuses it unless the result lies
79	strictly inside the blob root, both lexically and after following any
80	symlinks found in the data directory (the server never creates symlinks; a
81	symlink as the key itself is not treated as a blob).
82	
83	## Atomic writes
84	
85	`PUT` streams the body into `<data-dir>/tmp/<random>.part`, fsyncs it and
86	renames it to `<data-dir>/blobs/<key>`. Both live on the same mount (checked at
87	startup; the server refuses to start otherwise), so the rename atomically
88	replaces the old blob: a concurrent `GET` returns either the old or the new
89	contents in full, never a mix, and parallel `PUT`s to one key leave exactly one
90	complete version (the last rename wins). Files in `tmp/` are never listed or
91	reachable by any key. A failed or aborted upload removes its temp file;
92	leftovers from a crash are removed at the next startup, and unfinished uploads
93	are discarded on shutdown.
94	
95	## Layout
96	
97	| Path                   | Purpose                                                   |
98	| ---------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
99	| `src/server-main.js`   | server entry point (args, data dir checks, signals)       |
100	| `src/config.js`        | flag/env parsing                                          |
101	| `src/server.js`        | HTTP routing and handlers                                 |
102	| `src/blobs.js`         | blob key validation and on-disk storage                   |
103	| `bin/syncbox`          | client executable (runs `src/client-main.js`)             |
104	| `src/client-config.js` | client argument parsing                                   |
105	| `src/client.js`        | HTTP client for the server API                            |
106	| `src/local-files.js`   | walking and hashing the local directory                   |
107	| `src/push.js`          | the push command                                          |
108	| `src/pull.js`          | the pull command                                          |
109	| `test/`                | `node:test` suites (unit + spawned-process integration)   |
110	| `Dockerfile`           | `server`, `client` and `test` build targets               |
111	| `compose.yaml`         | the only place where Docker build/run options are defined |
112	| `run-server`, `run-client`, `run-tests` | wrappers required by the spec            |
